        "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14131901/observations/lesson-step-8748-3ecd12f8ab04e4c4c5ef.html",
        "screenshot": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14131901/observations/lesson-step-8748-3ecd12f8ab04e4c4c5ef.png",
        "source_url": "https://mathacademy.com/topics/1509",
        "captured_at": 1791785284.8748431,
        "math_academy_id": 2178,
        "title": "Introduction",
        "content": "Some angles have special names.\n\n- A **right angle** has a measure of $90^\\circ.$ It corresponds to one-quarter of a full turn.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14131901/assets/155a1d6d2abd868b05674dec47ef4b20a5147d30cd328a5ba53d00dea271d65c.png)\n\n- A **straight angle**, which is just a straight line, has a measure of $180^\\circ.$ It corresponds to one-half of a full turn.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14131901/assets/2423950f80de8354aee74c4f1daa6e42cf376caa0365307c76ebc7e09f96c8cf.png)\n\n- A **null angle** has a measure of $0^\\circ.$ You can imagine \"closing\" the angle until the two sides of the angle overlap. In this case, we don't have any turn, so its measure is zero!\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14131901/assets/f86b7c1b7ba758fb6b07b6aeabd03395d29103b45e4cd220444b675f70a6e630.png)\n\n- A **full angle** has a measure of $360^\\circ.$ You can imagine \"opening\" the angle until one of the sides goes all the way around. So, this is the angle between a ray and itself after it has made one full turn.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14131901/assets/cbc9e288f4d3905357539a221954328ef0ac799531fab62f034364d32a7c0f29.png)\n\nOn a diagram, null and full angles can look the same, so it is up to us what we choose to call it."
      }
    ],
